.TH "bash\-server\-token" "5" "2026\-02\-01" "bash\-server 0.1.0" "bash\-server file formats"
.SH DESCRIPTION
The \f[B]token file\f[R] contains the shared secret used to authenticate
clients connecting to \f[B]bash\-server\f[R](1).
The server generates a cryptographically random token at startup and
writes it to this file.
Clients must present the token via the \f[B]AUTH\f[R] command (v1) or
\f[B]auth\f[R] message (v2) before any other operations are permitted.
.PP
The token is deleted when the server shuts down cleanly.
If the server terminates abnormally, a stale token file may remain on
disk and must be removed manually before restarting.
.SH FORMAT
The file contains exactly one line: a \f[B]64\-character hexadecimal
string\f[R] encoding a 256\-bit (32\-byte) random value, followed by a
newline character (LF, 0x0A).
.IP
.EX
<64 hex digits>\(rsn
.EE
.PP
The hex digits use lowercase \f[B]a\f[R]\-\f[B]f\f[R].
No whitespace, prefix, or other content is present.
.SH File permissions
The token file is created with mode \f[B]0600\f[R] (owner read/write
only).
The server refuses to start if it cannot set these permissions.
Clients running as a different user will be unable to read the file,
which is the intended security boundary.
.SH File location
The token file path is derived from the socket path by appending
\f[B].token\f[R] to it:
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
lw(33.5n) lw(36.5n).
T{
Transport
T}@T{
Token path
T}
_
T{
Unix socket at \f[I]PATH\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[I]PATH\f[R]\f[B].token\f[R]
T}
T{
Named pipe with name \f[I]NAME\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[B]$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/bash\-server/\f[R]\f[I]NAME\f[R]\f[B].token\f[R]
T}
T{
Named pipe (no XDG)
T}@T{
\f[B]/tmp/bash\-server\-\f[R]\f[I]<uid>\f[R]\f[B]/\f[R]\f[I]NAME\f[R]\f[B].token\f[R]
T}
.TE
.PP
For example, if the socket is
\f[B]/run/user/1000/bash\-server/sock\f[R], the token file is
\f[B]/run/user/1000/bash\-server/sock.token\f[R].
.SH Lifecycle
.IP "1." 3
\f[B]Startup\f[R]: server reads 32 bytes from \f[B]/dev/urandom\f[R],
hex\-encodes them, and writes the result to the token file.
.IP "2." 3
\f[B]Runtime\f[R]: clients read the file and send the token with their
first protocol message.
.IP "3." 3
\f[B]Shutdown\f[R]: server unlinks the token file.
If the socket file is also being removed, the token file is deleted
first.
.SH EXAMPLES
A typical token file:
.IP
.EX
a3f7c9e2b14d08563fa91e7c4b2d60f8e5a3c7d9012b4f68e3a1c5d7f9b20e46
.EE
.PP
Reading the token in a shell script:
.IP
.EX
TOKEN=$(cat /run/user/1000/bash\-server/sock.token)
.EE
.PP
Verifying file permissions:
.IP
.EX
stat \-c \(aq%a\(aq /run/user/1000/bash\-server/sock.token
\f[I]# Expected output: 600\f[R]
.EE
.SH SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
The token provides the sole authentication mechanism for bash\-server.
Anyone who can read the token file can execute arbitrary commands in the
server\(cqs Bash session.
Therefore:
.IP \(bu 2
The file MUST remain mode \f[B]0600\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
The containing directory SHOULD be mode \f[B]0700\f[R] or
\f[B]0750\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
The token MUST be generated from a cryptographic random source
(\f[B]/dev/urandom\f[R]).
.IP \(bu 2
Constant\-time comparison (\f[B]secure_compare\f[R]) is used
server\-side to prevent timing attacks.
